  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Computer Science<br><span>Glossary of Key Terms</span></h1>
    <p>Alphabetically sorted definitions of essential computer science concepts, from algorithms to theory of computation.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Definitions</h2>
    </div>

    <dl>
      <dt>Algorithm</dt>
      <dd>A finite, well-defined sequence of steps to solve a problem or perform a computation. Characterized by correctness, efficiency, and clarity. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Asymptotic Notation</dt>
      <dd>Mathematical notation describing limiting behavior of functions. Big-O (upper bound), Big-Ω (lower bound), Big-Θ (tight bound) for complexity analysis. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Automaton</dt>
      <dd>An abstract computing model with states and transitions. Includes finite automata, pushdown automata, and Turing machines. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/theory-of-computation">Theory of Computation</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Binary Search</dt>
      <dd>A divide-and-conquer algorithm finding an element in a sorted array by repeatedly halving the search space. O(log n) time complexity. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Binary Tree</dt>
      <dd>A tree data structure where each node has at most two children (left and right). Variants include BST, AVL, and heap. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Boolean Logic</dt>
      <dd>A form of algebra using AND, OR, and NOT operations on true/false values. Foundation of digital circuits and conditional expressions. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/discrete-mathematics">Discrete Mathematics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Breadth-First Search (BFS)</dt>
      <dd>A graph traversal algorithm exploring all neighbors at the current depth before moving deeper. Uses a queue. Finds shortest paths in unweighted graphs. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Cache</dt>
      <dd>A fast, small memory store for frequently accessed data. Reduces average memory access time. Types include L1, L2, L3, and disk cache. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/operating-systems">Operating Systems</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Complexity Class</dt>
      <dd>A set of decision problems solvable by abstract machines under specific resource bounds. Major classes: P, NP, PSPACE, EXPTIME. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/theory-of-computation">Theory of Computation</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Context-Free Grammar</dt>
      <dd>A formal grammar with production rules of the form A → α. Generates context-free languages recognized by pushdown automata. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/theory-of-computation">Theory of Computation</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Cryptographic Hash</dt>
      <dd>A one-way function mapping input to fixed-size output. Properties: collision resistance, preimage resistance, avalanche effect. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/computer-networks">Computer Networks</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Data Structure</dt>
      <dd>A specialized format for organizing, processing, and storing data efficiently. Examples: arrays, linked lists, trees, graphs, hash tables. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Database Normalization</dt>
      <dd>The process of organizing database tables to minimize redundancy. Normal forms: 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/databases">Databases</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Depth-First Search (DFS)</dt>
      <dd>A graph traversal algorithm exploring as far as possible along each branch before backtracking. Uses a stack (or recursion). See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Dijkstra's Algorithm</dt>
      <dd>An algorithm finding the shortest path from a source node to all other nodes in a weighted graph with non-negative edges. O((V + E) log V) with priority queue. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Encryption</dt>
      <dd>The process of converting plaintext into ciphertext using an algorithm and key. Types: symmetric (shared key) and asymmetric (public/private key). See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/computer-networks">Computer Networks</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Finite Automaton</dt>
      <dd>A computational model with finite states and transitions on input symbols. Recognizes regular languages. Deterministic (DFA) and nondeterministic (NFA) variants. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/theory-of-computation">Theory of Computation</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Graph</dt>
      <dd>A mathematical structure with vertices (nodes) and edges (connections). Directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted. Models networks, relationships, and dependencies. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/discrete-mathematics">Discrete Mathematics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Hash Table</dt>
      <dd>A data structure mapping keys to values using a hash function. Provides average O(1) lookup, insertion, and deletion. Handles collisions via chaining or open addressing. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Heapsort</dt>
      <dd>A comparison-based sorting algorithm using a binary heap data structure. O(n log n) time complexity, in-place. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)</dt>
      <dd>An application-layer protocol for transmitting hypermedia documents. Request-response model between clients and servers. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/computer-networks">Computer Networks</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Index</dt>
      <dd>A data structure improving query performance on database tables. B-tree and hash indexes are most common. Trades write speed for read speed. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/databases">Databases</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Interrupt</dt>
      <dd>A signal to the processor indicating an event requiring immediate attention. Enables multitasking and hardware communication. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/operating-systems">Operating Systems</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Linked List</dt>
      <dd>A linear data structure where elements are stored in nodes with pointers to next (and optionally previous) nodes. O(1) insertion/deletion, O(n) access. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Logic Gate</dt>
      <dd>A physical device implementing Boolean functions. Basic gates: AND, OR, NOT, NAND, NOR, XOR. Foundation of digital circuits. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/discrete-mathematics">Discrete Mathematics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Merge Sort</dt>
      <dd>A divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm splitting the array, recursively sorting halves, and merging. O(n log n) time, stable, requires O(n) space. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>NP-Complete</dt>
      <dd>A class of decision problems where any problem in NP can be polynomial-time reduced to them. Finding polynomial solutions would prove P = NP. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/theory-of-computation">Theory of Computation</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)</dt>
      <dd>A programming paradigm organizing code around objects combining data and behavior. Core principles: encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction. See <a href="/topics/programming">Programming</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Process</dt>
      <dd>A program in execution with its own memory space, registers, and state. Managed by the OS scheduler. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/operating-systems">Operating Systems</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Quicksort</dt>
      <dd>A divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm partitioning around a pivot element. Average O(n log n), worst case O(n²). In-place but not stable. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Recursion</dt>
      <dd>A technique where a function calls itself with smaller inputs. Base case terminates; recursive case progresses toward it. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Red-Black Tree</dt>
      <dd>A self-balancing binary search tree with nodes colored red or black. Guarantees O(log n) operations. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>SQL (Structured Query Language)</dt>
      <dd>A declarative language for managing relational databases. Supports SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE operations. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/databases">Databases</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Stack</dt>
      <dd>A LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) data structure supporting push and pop operations. Used in function calls, expression evaluation, and backtracking. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Thread</dt>
      <dd>The smallest unit of CPU execution within a process. Multiple threads share the same address space. Enables concurrent execution. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/operating-systems">Operating Systems</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Time Complexity</dt>
      <dd>A measure of how runtime grows with input size. Common classes: O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), O(2ⁿ). See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Tournament Tree</dt>
      <dd>A tree structure for finding the minimum or maximum element efficiently. Used in selection algorithms and external sorting. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/algorithms-and-data-structures">Algorithms & Data Structures</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Transaction</dt>
      <dd>A sequence of database operations treated as a single unit with ACID properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/databases">Databases</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Turing Machine</dt>
      <dd>An abstract computational model with an infinite tape, read/write head, and state register. Defines the Church-Turing thesis for computability. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/theory-of-computation">Theory of Computation</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Virtual Memory</dt>
      <dd>Memory management technique mapping virtual addresses to physical addresses. Enables programs to use more memory than physically available via paging. See <a href="https://computer-science.wyattau.com/docs/computer-science/operating-systems">Operating Systems</a>.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

